\chapter{Reaction Core Thermodynamics}

\section{Overview}
The Reaction Core Vessel of the Matter Jet Mk-30 operates under extreme
thermodynamic conditions, including relativistic plasma temperatures,
ultra-high radiation flux, and petawatt-scale energy conversion. This chapter
develops the full thermodynamic framework governing the core, including
enthalpy transport, radiation pressure, baryonic mass injection, and
micro-channel heat extraction.

\section{Thermodynamic State Variables}
The relativistic plasma inside the reaction core is characterized by:
\begin{align}
T_e &\approx 1.42 \times 10^8~\text{K}, \\
n_e &\approx 4.18 \times 10^{22}~\text{m}^{-3}, \\
p &= n_e k_B T_e,
\end{align}
where $T_e$ is electron temperature and $n_e$ is electron density.

The pressure is
\begin{equation}
p \approx 8.2 \times 10^{14}~\text{Pa}.
\end{equation}

\section{Relativistic Enthalpy Density}
The enthalpy density is
\begin{equation}
h = \rho c^2 + p,
\end{equation}
where $\rho$ is mass density.

For the Mk-30 plasma,
\[
h \approx \rho c^2 + 8.2 \times 10^{14}~\text{Pa}.
\]

The enthalpy flux is
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{H} = h \gamma^2 \mathbf{v}.
\end{equation}

\section{Radiation Pressure and Energy Density}
The radiation energy density is
\begin{equation}
u_{\text{rad}} = a T^4,
\end{equation}
where $a$ is the radiation constant.

For $T = 1.42 \times 10^8$ K,
\[
u_{\text{rad}} \approx 4.1 \times 10^{25}~\text{J/m}^3.
\]

The radiation pressure is
\begin{equation}
p_{\text{rad}} = \frac{1}{3} u_{\text{rad}}.
\end{equation}

\section{Thermal Radiation Flux}
The radiative flux from the core is
\begin{equation}
F_{\text{rad}} = \sigma T^4,
\end{equation}
where $\sigma$ is the Stefan-Boltzmann constant.

For the Mk-30,
\[
F_{\text{rad}} \approx 1.2 \times 10^{24}~\text{W/m}^2.
\]

This flux is confined by the W-Re liner and extracted by GaIn micro-channel
cooling.

\section{Baryonic Mass Injection Thermodynamics}
The baryonic aerosol injection rate is
\[
\dot{m} = 12.98~\text{g/s}.
\]

The injected mass undergoes:
\begin{itemize}
\item rapid ionization,
\item relativistic acceleration,
\item magnetic confinement,
\item enthalpy conversion.
\end{itemize}

The ionization energy is negligible compared to relativistic kinetic energy.

\section{Relativistic Heat Generation}
The total heat generated is
\begin{equation}
Q_{\text{total}} = (1 - \eta) P_0,
\end{equation}
where $\eta = 0.95$.

Thus,
\[
Q_{\text{total}} = 5.0 \times 10^{14}~\text{W}.
\]

This heat must be extracted continuously.

\section{Micro-Channel Heat Extraction}
The GaIn coolant absorbs heat according to
\begin{equation}
Q = \dot{m}_{\text{coolant}} c_p \Delta T.
\end{equation}

For the Mk-30,
\[
\Delta T = 412.4~\text{K}.
\]

The micro-channel geometry maximizes surface area and minimizes thermal
resistance.

\section{CNT Radiator Thermodynamics}
The CNT radiator arrays operate at
\[
T_{\text{eff}} \approx 5450~\text{K}.
\]

The radiated power is
\begin{equation}
P_{\text{rad}} = \epsilon \sigma A T_{\text{eff}}^4,
\end{equation}
where $\epsilon \approx 1$ for CNT surfaces.

The spectrum peaks at
\[
\lambda_{\text{peak}} \approx 531~\text{nm},
\]
matching solar photosphere emission.

\section{Thermal Stress and Material Limits}
The SiC matrix and W-Re liner must withstand:
\begin{itemize}
\item extreme radiation flux,
\item relativistic plasma pressure,
\item thermal shock,
\item magnetic stress.
\end{itemize}

The thermal stress is
\begin{equation}
\sigma_{\text{thermal}} = E \alpha \Delta T,
\end{equation}
where $E$ is Young’s modulus and $\alpha$ is thermal expansion coefficient.

SiC and W-Re remain within safe limits.

\section{Summary}
The Reaction Core Vessel of the Mk-30 operates under extreme thermodynamic
conditions, including:
\begin{itemize}
\item $10^{16}$ W energy envelope,
\item $10^8$ K plasma temperatures,
\item $10^{25}$ J/m$^3$ radiation energy density,
\item 500 TW waste heat,
\item relativistic enthalpy flux.
\end{itemize}

The thermodynamic architecture is stable due to:
\begin{itemize}
\item SiC/W-Re structural materials,
\item GaIn micro-channel cooling,
\item CNT radiators,
\item relativistic enthalpy routing.
\end{itemize}

This chapter establishes the thermodynamic basis for the superconducting magnet
architecture developed in Chapter 5.

